┌───────────────────────────────────────┐  
│ Introduction                           │  
│                                       │  
│ Uniform acceleration describes the   │  
│ constant change in velocity of an     │  
│ object over equal intervals of time. │  
│ It is a cornerstone concept in        │  
│ kinematics, allowing us to predict     │  
│ motion with simple linear equations. │  
└───────────────────────────────────────┘  

┌───────────────────────────────────────┐  
│ Definition                             │  
│                                       │  
│ **Uniform acceleration** is the       │  
│ constant rate at which an object's     │  
│ velocity changes.  Mathematically,     │  
│ it means the acceleration *a* has a   │  
│ single value for the entire motion.   │  
└───────────────────────────────────────┘  

┌───────────────────────────────────────┐  
│ Key Concepts                           │  
│                                       │  
│ • **Δv (Δv)** – Change in velocity.   │  
│ • **Δt (Δt)** – Change in time.       │  
│ • **a** – Acceleration, measured in   │  
│   meters per second squared (m/s²).   │  
│ • When *a* is constant, the motion     │  
│   follows a straight‑line relationship │  
│   between velocity and time.           │  
└───────────────────────────────────────┘  

┌───────────────────────────────────────┐  
│ Formula                                │  
│                                       │  
│ ┌─────────────────────────────────────┐ │  
│ │ Main Formula                         │ │  
│ │                                     │ │  
│ │   a = Δv / Δt                       │ │  
│ │                                     │ │  
│ │ [ Explain Formula ]                 │ │  
│ │ [ Open Formula Lab ]                 │ │  
│ └─────────────────────────────────────┘ │  
└───────────────────────────────────────┘  

┌───────────────────────────────────────┐  
│ Applications                           │  
│                                       │  
│ • Predicting the speed of a car after │  
│   a set time when it accelerates        │  
│   uniformly.                           │  
│ • Calculating stopping distances for   │  
│   vehicles (using the related formula   │  
│   *v² = u² + 2as*).                     │  
│ • Determining the motion of projectiles│  
│   when air resistance is negligible.   │  
└───────────────────────────────────────┘  

┌───────────────────────────────────────┐  
│ Advantages                             │  
│                                       │  
│ • Simple linear relationship makes     │  
│   calculations straightforward.        │  
│ • Provides a good approximation for   │  
│   short‑time intervals where forces are │  
│   nearly constant.                     │  
└───────────────────────────────────────┘  

┌───────────────────────────────────────┐  
│ Disadvantages                          │  
│                                       │  
│ • Real‑world motion often involves      │  
│   varying forces, so the constant‑a     │  
│   assumption may be inaccurate over     │  
│   long periods.                         │  
│ • Ignores effects such as air drag and   │  
│   friction unless they are explicitly   │  
│   accounted for.                        │  
└───────────────────────────────────────┘  

┌───────────────────────────────────────┐  
│ Important Notes                        │  
│                                       │  
│ • Uniform acceleration **does not**    │  
│   require the motion to start from     │  
│   rest; the initial velocity *u* can be │  
│   any value.                            │  
│ • The formula can be rearranged to      │  
│   solve for any missing variable:       │  
│   Δv = a·Δt   or   Δt = Δv / a.          │  
│ • Units must be consistent (e.g.,      │  
│   meters, seconds).                    │  
└───────────────────────────────────────┘  

┌───────────────────────────────────────┐  
│ Units Table                            │  
│                                       │  
│ +----------------+-----------+----------+ │  
│ | Variable       | Symbol    | Unit     | │  
│ +----------------+-----------+----------+ │  
│ | Acceleration   | a         | m/s²    | │  
│ | Velocity change| Δv        | m/s     | │  
│ | Time change    | Δt        | s       | │  
│ +----------------+-----------+----------+ │  
│                                       │  
└───────────────────────────────────────┘  

┌───────────────────────────────────────┐  
│ Summary                                │  
│                                       │  
│ Uniform acceleration is a constant    │  
│ rate of velocity change, expressed by   │  
│ *a = Δv / Δt*.  It enables quick        │  
│ predictions of motion when forces stay  │  
│ unchanged, though it is an idealized    │  
│ model for many real‑world situations.   │  
└───────────────────────────────────────┘  

┌───────────────────────────────────────┐  
│ Suggested Questions                    │  
│                                       │  
│ 1. If a car accelerates uniformly from │  
│    0 to 20 m/s in 5 s, what is its      │  
│    acceleration?                       │  
│ 2. A cyclist slows down from 15 m/s to │  
│    5 m/s over 4 s. What is the          │  
│    acceleration (or deceleration)?    │  
│ 3. How would you modify the formula if │  
│    acceleration varies with time?      │  
│ 4. Why might the uniform‑acceleration   │  
│    model fail for a falling object?    │  
└───────────────────────────────────────┘  